At present, builds require GNU make (standard with Linux, OSX-FINK, cygwin on windows).
Windows users - study especially README_WINDOWS

1)  Define the environment variable $CSIRO_OSTYPE
    for platform specific identification. Examples:
    Linux-i686-unknown, Darwin-Power-Macintosh-unknown (for MacOSX) etc.
    We contrive this from the output of 'uname -smp'

    [csh]% setenv CSIRO_OSTYPE `uname -smp | sed 's/ /-/g;s/-[^-]*$/-unknown/g'`
    [sh]%  export CSIRO_OSTYPE=`uname -smp | sed 's/ /-/g;s/-[^-]*$/-unknown/g'`

    On common 32 bit intel Linuxes, you get:  $CSIRO_OSTYPE = Linux-i686-unknown

    Set this variable permanently in your .cshrc or .profile

2) To build, platform specific things need to be defined in a file
   Makefile.${CSIRO_OSTYPE} see, e.g. the example for Linux;
   Makefile.Linux-i686-unknown.  Working examples are there for Linux
   32 & 64 bit, OSX PPC and intel, and 'doze via mingw/cygwin.

   The c and fortran flags are those required to build a "java native
   interface" (JNI) shared library, as per the acres of public
   documentation for JNI (Google "jni tutorial" etc). Note the fortran
   is mixed 77/90, mainly to glue a kind of dynamic memory management
   (from java) onto the f77 original computational kernels. Copy an
   existing file, and set the compiler & linker flags suitably.

3) Type
   % make
   and things should build.
   To run the code, see README_RUNNING

